===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-mix up.ogg|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|idiomatic}} To [[mix]] or [[blend]] thoroughly and completely.
#: {{syn|en|scramble|jumble}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''mixed up''' peat moss, humus, and compost to make potting soil.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|idiomatic}} To [[combine]] thoroughly.
#: {{ux|en|All the smells of the food had '''mixed up''' together.}}
# {{senseid|en|prepare (something) by mixing}} {{lb|en|transitive|idiomatic}} To [[prepare]] something from [[ingredient]]s that are mixed.
#: {{troponyms|en|whip up}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''mixed up''' a batch of her own potting soil.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1956|author=Katherine Lever|chapter={{w|Aristophanes}}, Comic Poet|title=The Art of Greek Comedy|location=London|publisher=[[w:Methuen Publishing|Methuen & Co.]]{{nb...|Ltd., 36 Essex Street, Strand, London W.C.2}}|page=140|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/artofgreekcomedy0000kath/page/140/mode/1up|oclc=307494|passage=The picture of their life that follows suggests in a few words a country kitchen—the sizzling of fish over the charcoal fire, fanned by the bellows to a hot flame, the '''mixing up''' of a Thasian sauce for the fish, the kneading of dough.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|idiomatic}} To [[confuse]] or [[reverse]].
#: {{syn|en|jumble|Thesaurus:confuse}}
#: {{ux|en|I always '''mix up''' Vermont and New Hampshire on a map.}}
#: {{ux|en|I always '''mix up''' Jack with Jake.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|idiomatic}} To [[confuse]], [[befuddle]] (a person).
# {{senseid|en|involve socially etc}} {{lb|en|transitive|usually|passive|with ''with''|often|with ''be'' or ''get''}} To [[become]] [[involved]] with, especially socially or romantically.
#: {{syn|en|embroil}}
#: {{ux|en|He got '''mixed up''' with a bad crowd.}}
#: {{ux|en|Then he got '''mixed up''' with an older woman.}}
#: {{ux|en|Now, he's '''mixed up''' with some half-baked religion.}}
# To [[shuffle]].
#: {{ux|en|'''mix up''' this Rubik's cube}}

